add(slug="how-to-block-reels-on-android", tag="How-to", mins=6,
 title="How to Block Reels on Android (Instagram, Facebook and YouTube Shorts)",
 seo="How to Block Reels on Android: Instagram, Facebook, Shorts",
 desc="No Android setting turns off Reels or Shorts. Here are the options that do work, what each one costs you, and how to cap short videos without losing your chats.",
 keywords="how to block reels on android, block reels, turn off reels, disable reels android, block shorts android, stop reels, reels blocker app",
 related=["instagram-reels-blocker-guide","youtube-shorts-limiter-guide","how-to-use-digital-wellbeing-android"],
 lede="People ask this a lot, usually after an evening they didn't mean to lose. The short answer: Android has no switch for it, and neither does Instagram. There are still a few ways to get there, and they're not all equally annoying.",
 body='''
<h2>Why there's no off switch</h2>
<p>Reels, Shorts and Facebook's version are the stickiest parts of those apps, so the apps put them everywhere: a tab, the home feed, search, even inside Stories. You can say "not interested" to a video, but that only changes what you see next. It doesn't remove the feature.</p>
<h2>Option 1: Delete the app and use the browser</h2>
<p>It works, in the sense that the mobile site is clunky enough to put you off. You lose notifications and some features, and plenty of people reinstall within a week because they miss the messages. Fine if you only use the app to scroll. Not great if you actually talk to people on it.</p>
<h2>Option 2: A timer on the whole app</h2>
<p>Android's Digital Wellbeing can pause an app after a set time each day. Handy for a game. For Instagram or YouTube it tends to backfire, because the timer ends your messages and tutorials too, so you dismiss it and carry on. I wrote more about that in <a href="how-to-use-digital-wellbeing-android.html">how to use Digital Wellbeing</a>.</p>
<h2>Option 3: Limit just the short videos</h2>
<p>This is what Brainrot: Reel Limit does. It counts each Reel or Short as you swipe, and when you hit your daily number it closes that feed. Messages, Stories, profiles and long videos aren't touched.</p>
<ol>
<li>Install the app from Google Play.</li>
<li>Switch on its Accessibility permission (Settings → Accessibility). This is how it knows a Reel is open and when you swipe. <a href="../privacy.html">Here's what it can and can't see.</a></li>
<li>Pick a number. Zero is a full block. If you're not ready for that, start at roughly 25% below what you do now.</li>
<li>Choose one shared limit across all apps, or a separate one for each.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><strong>Worth knowing</strong>Instagram and YouTube update their apps a lot. Once in a while a layout change breaks detection until we ship a fix. If counting stops, update both apps and <a href="../contact.html">tell us</a>.</div>
<h2>Which one should you pick?</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>If you…</th><th>Try</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Only use the app to scroll</td><td>Delete it, use the browser</td></tr>
<tr><td>Need the app for chats or learning</td><td>A per-video limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mostly lose time at night</td><td>A limit plus a bedtime rule, see <a href="stop-watching-reels-at-night.html">stopping Reels at night</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table>''')

add(slug="are-reels-addictive", tag="Explainer", mins=6,
 title="Are Reels Addictive? An Honest Answer",
 seo="Are Reels Addictive? What We Know (and Don't)",
 desc="Reels and Shorts are built to be hard to stop, but 'addictive' isn't a clean yes or no. What makes them sticky, who struggles most, and when to worry.",
 keywords="are reels addictive, reels addiction, short video addiction, is instagram reels addictive, shorts addiction, why are reels so addictive",
 related=["short-form-video-addiction-effects","what-is-brainrot","how-many-reels-a-day-is-too-many"],
 lede="\"Is it addiction?\" is a bigger question than it sounds. Clinically, probably not for most people. But \"is it designed to be hard to put down?\" has a much shorter answer: yes.",
 body='''
<h2>What makes them so sticky</h2>
<p>Three ingredients do most of the work.</p>
<p><strong>Unpredictable reward.</strong> Most videos are fine, a few are great, and you can't tell which is which until you've watched. That uncertainty is a strong hook. It's the same basic idea behind slot machines.</p>
<p><strong>No stopping point.</strong> A book has chapters. A TV show has credits. A feed just keeps going, and autoplay makes the choice for you.</p>
<p><strong>Almost no effort.</strong> One thumb, no decisions. Continuing is easier than stopping, which is the opposite of how most good habits work.</p>
<h2>So is it an addiction?</h2>
<p>"Addiction" is a clinical word. Researchers who study this tend to use "problematic use" instead, meaning use that feels out of your control and starts to cost you something: sleep, work, mood, relationships. Short-video apps aren't classified as an addiction in the way gambling disorder is, and the research is still catching up.</p>
<p>What's well documented is that plenty of people say they use these apps more than they want to. That gap, between what you meant to do and what you did, is the part worth paying attention to.</p>
<h2>Why some people struggle more</h2>
<p>People who are bored, stressed, lonely or very tired reach for feeds more often. People with ADHD often say feeds are especially hard to leave (we wrote about that <a href="adhd-screen-time-management.html">here</a>). And younger users, whose habits are still forming, tend to report more trouble. None of that is a character flaw. It's how the product meets the person.</p>
<h2>A quick self-check</h2>
<ul>
<li>Do you often watch far more than you planned?</li>
<li>Have you tried to cut back and failed?</li>
<li>Do you lose sleep over it?</li>
<li>Do you feel worse after a session than before?</li>
</ul>
<p>Two or more "yes" answers don't mean something's wrong with you. They do suggest a limit would help. If it's affecting your health, studies or relationships, talking to a doctor or counsellor is a good idea, whatever the label.</p>
<p class="disclaimer">General information, not medical advice.</p>''')

add(slug="stop-watching-reels-at-night", tag="Sleep", mins=6,
 title="How to Stop Watching Reels at Night and Actually Sleep",
 seo="How to Stop Watching Reels at Night (Sleep Better)",
 desc="Late-night Reels quietly eat your sleep. What helps: an evening cutoff, moving the charger, and a daily limit that ends the session for you.",
 keywords="stop watching reels at night, reels before bed, doomscrolling at night, scrolling in bed, sleep and phone use, how to stop scrolling before bed",
 related=["how-to-stop-doomscrolling","dopamine-detox-guide","how-many-reels-a-day-is-too-many"],
 lede="The pattern is familiar. You get into bed at 11, tell yourself you'll check one thing, and it's suddenly past midnight. Willpower is at its lowest exactly when the feed is at its most tempting, so this is one of the easiest places to fix with a rule instead of effort.",
 body='''
<h2>What it actually costs</h2>
<p>The main cost isn't some magic property of the screen. It's time. Every extra half hour of scrolling is half an hour off your sleep, and it's hard to get that back. Bright, stimulating content right before bed can also make it harder to wind down, though how much varies from person to person.</p>
<h2>Changes that tend to work</h2>
<h3>Move the charger</h3>
<p>Put your phone on the other side of the room, or at least out of arm's reach. If you can't grab it lazily, you're much less likely to start. Use a cheap alarm clock for waking up.</p>
<h3>Pick a cutoff, and make it earlier than feels necessary</h3>
<p>Aim for 30 to 60 minutes of no short videos before sleep. Even 20 minutes is better than nothing. Choose a time and set a phone alarm labelled "wind down".</p>
<h3>Give yourself something else to do in bed</h3>
<p>A paperback, a podcast with a sleep timer, or just a few pages of a notebook. A gap with nothing in it gets filled by the phone.</p>
<h3>Use bedtime mode</h3>
<p>On most Android phones, Digital Wellbeing has a Bedtime mode that turns the screen greyscale and silences notifications on a schedule. Grey feeds are noticeably less fun.</p>
<h3>Let a limit end the session for you</h3>
<p>If you do scroll at night, set a small daily cap so the feed closes on its own. With Brainrot: Reel Limit, a lot of people set the number lower than they think they need. By the time you reach it, you've usually had enough anyway. The difference is that you don't have to be the one who stops.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>A small experiment</strong>Try one week with no short videos after 10pm. Note when you fall asleep and how you feel in the morning. Your own numbers will convince you better than any article.</div>''')

add(slug="how-many-reels-a-day-is-too-many", tag="Habits", mins=5,
 title="How Many Reels a Day Is Too Many?",
 seo="How Many Reels a Day Is Too Many? A Practical Answer",
 desc="There's no official limit, but there is a useful way to think about it. A bit of maths, a few honest questions, and sensible daily numbers to start from.",
 keywords="how many reels a day, reels time limit, how long should i watch reels, daily reels limit, how many shorts a day, safe screen time reels",
 related=["are-reels-addictive","how-to-stop-doomscrolling","how-to-block-reels-on-android"],
 lede="Nobody has published a safe number of Reels per day, and anyone who gives you one with total confidence is guessing. What you can do is work out what your number costs, and decide if it's a good trade.",
 body='''
<h2>Start with the maths</h2>
<p>Short videos vary a lot in length, but 30 seconds is a fair guess. At that length:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Reels per day</th><th>Roughly</th><th>Over a year</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>30</td><td>15 minutes</td><td>about 91 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>100</td><td>50 minutes</td><td>about 300 hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>250</td><td>2 hours</td><td>about 760 hours</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>It's a rough guide, since actual time depends on video length and how long you linger. But it makes the point: a number that sounds small can be a lot of hours once you multiply it out.</p>
<h2>The better question</h2>
<p>Instead of "what's the right number", ask what those hours would otherwise be. If 100 Reels replaces a commute you'd spend staring at nothing, that's one thing. If it replaces sleep, study or time with people you like, it's another.</p>
<h2>Some starting points</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Studying for something important:</strong> zero, for a while.</li>
<li><strong>Normal weeks:</strong> 20 to 40 is where many people land. Enough for a real break.</li>
<li><strong>Currently at 200+ a day:</strong> don't jump to 20. Halve it, hold it for a week, then halve again.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to find your real number</h2>
<p>Most people guess low. Count for two days without trying to change anything, then set your limit about a quarter below that. The Brainrot app keeps a 7-day history for exactly this reason, but any tally works. After a week or two, drop it a little more.</p>
<p>If you keep hitting your cap by lunchtime, the number is too low or the cravings are telling you something. Adjust it, don't fight it.</p>''')

add(slug="screen-time-for-students", tag="Students", mins=7,
 title="Screen Time for Students: A Realistic Study-Week Plan",
 seo="Screen Time for Students: A Realistic Study-Week Plan",
 desc="A study plan that doesn't pretend you can live without your phone. How to keep lectures and group chats while shutting out Reels and Shorts during exam weeks.",
 keywords="screen time for students, reduce phone use while studying, study without phone distraction, exam preparation phone addiction, block reels while studying, student focus tips",
 related=["why-willpower-fails-against-algorithms","how-to-reduce-screen-time-android","dopamine-detox-guide"],
 lede="Telling a student to \"just put the phone away\" ignores that the phone is also the lecture, the group chat and the doubt-clearing channel. A plan that works has to keep the useful parts and cut the rest.",
 body='''
<h2>What actually eats study time</h2>
<p>It's rarely the big, planned distraction. It's the two-minute break that turns into forty, usually in a Reels or Shorts feed. Longer videos at least end. Feeds don't.</p>
<h2>A plan for a normal study week</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Decide your study windows.</strong> For example 6 to 9pm on weekdays and a morning block on Sunday. Fixed times beat "whenever I feel like it".</li>
<li><strong>Keep the phone where you can't reach it.</strong> Another room if you can. In a bag across the room if you can't.</li>
<li><strong>Allow only what you need.</strong> Phone, messages, and the one or two apps your course uses. Everything else waits.</li>
<li><strong>Use a short-video cap, not a ban, in normal weeks.</strong> 15 to 25 a day lets you have a break without losing the evening.</li>
<li><strong>Go to zero for exam week.</strong> It's only for a few days, and the first two are the hardest.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Where Brainrot fits</h2>
<p>You can schedule focus sessions for those study windows so they start by themselves, with your essential apps still available. And you can set the Reels and Shorts limit to 0 for exam week while lectures on YouTube still play. More on the <a href="../students.html">students page</a>.</p>
<h2>Breaks that don't wreck the session</h2>
<p>Work in blocks of 25 to 50 minutes, then take a five or ten minute break that doesn't involve a feed. Walk, water, stretch, look out of a window. It sounds too simple to matter, but a break that ends on its own is what you're after.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>If it's not working</strong>Look at the numbers before blaming yourself. If you hit the limit at 4pm every day, you may need a lower baseline, an earlier cutoff or just more sleep.</div>''')

add(slug="how-to-use-digital-wellbeing-android", tag="How-to", mins=6,
 title="How to Use Digital Wellbeing on Android (and Where It Falls Short)",
 seo="How to Use Digital Wellbeing on Android: Setup & Limits",
 desc="A plain walkthrough of Android's Digital Wellbeing: app timers, Focus mode and Bedtime mode. What each does well, and where you'll need something extra.",
 keywords="digital wellbeing android, how to use digital wellbeing, set app timer android, focus mode android, bedtime mode android, digital wellbeing alternative",
 related=["how-to-reduce-screen-time-android","best-app-blockers-android","how-to-block-reels-on-android"],
 lede="Digital Wellbeing is already on most Android phones, it's free, and it needs no extra permissions. If you haven't tried it, start there. Here's how to use it, and an honest look at what it can't do.",
 body='''
<h2>Where to find it</h2>
<p>Open <strong>Settings → Digital Wellbeing &amp; parental controls</strong>. The exact name can differ a little between brands (Samsung, Xiaomi and others tweak it), but it's always in Settings.</p>
<h2>The dashboard</h2>
<p>The first screen shows today's screen time, a breakdown per app, notifications received and how many times you unlocked the phone. Look at a few days. You'll probably find one or two apps account for most of the time.</p>
<h2>App timers</h2>
<ol>
<li>On the dashboard, tap the hourglass icon next to an app.</li>
<li>Set a daily limit, for example 30 minutes.</li>
<li>When the time is up, the app is paused until midnight.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is great for apps you don't really need: a game, a shopping app, a news app. It's less good for apps that do two jobs.</p>
<h2>Focus mode</h2>
<p>Pick the apps that distract you, and Focus mode pauses them on demand or on a schedule. Useful for work hours. You can usually take a short break from it, which is both handy and, on a bad day, a weakness.</p>
<h2>Bedtime mode</h2>
<p>Schedules Do Not Disturb and can turn the screen greyscale at night. It makes feeds less appealing, and it's one of the better built-in features.</p>
<h2>Where it falls short</h2>
<p>The one thing Digital Wellbeing can't do is look inside an app. It knows you spent 70 minutes on Instagram. It doesn't know whether that was messaging a friend or watching 140 Reels. So the timer either hits everything or nothing, and you end up tapping "ignore limit".</p>
<p>That gap is why we built Brainrot: Reel Limit. It's meant to sit alongside Digital Wellbeing, not replace it: use the built-in tool for app timers and Bedtime mode, and a per-video limit for the feeds. There's a straightforward <a href="../comparison.html">comparison here</a>.</p>''')
